<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
    Project-wide SpotBugs exclude filter. Applied to every module in
    addition to that module's own spotbugs_ignore.xml (if any). Wired
    via the parent pom's `<excludeFilterFile>` reference in
    pluginManagement and the `spotbugs-module-ignore` profile.

    Only categories that are structurally false-positive in this
    project belong here. Module-specific suppressions (a single
    class/field tuple) stay in the per-module spotbugs_ignore.xml.
-->
<FindBugsFilter>

    <Match>
        <!-- The project does not use Java Object Serialization anywhere.
             Persisted state is Jackson JSON end-to-end (config files,
             remote-RPC wire format, validation reports, etc.). Swing
             components and Jackson types inherit Serializable transitively
             but no class is ever written via ObjectOutputStream.

             Non-Serializable instance fields are marked `transient` to
             silence the javac [serial] lint. SpotBugs then flags those
             transient fields as "not restored on deserialisation" — true
             but irrelevant: the deserialisation path is never exercised. -->
        <Bug pattern="SE_TRANSIENT_FIELD_NOT_RESTORED" />
    </Match>

    <Match>
        <!--
            Reported only since SpotBugs 4.10.0, which added *partial* JSpecify support:

              "Add partial support for org.jspecify.annotations.Nullable ... aliased to the
               closest existing SpotBugs nullness annotations. This is not a complete
               implementation of the JSpecify spec."

            This project completed a repo-wide JSpecify rollout, so 4.10 suddenly sees
            @Nullable annotations that 4.9.8.3 ignored entirely. Measured in THIS repository
            on 2026-09-02, with the SpotBugs bump to 4.10.4.0: cumba-oss-corej-core reports
            188 of this pattern where 4.9.8.3 reported none. They concentrate in the tolerant
            wire-format CDISC rule model, whose Lombok @Data getters return @Nullable fields:
            Rule 48, Operation 44, CheckConditionLeaf 23, RulePackage 15, Scope 9,
            MatchDataset 8.

            Those 188 were classified, not assumed: all 22 distinct callees are inside
            net.cumba (ZERO third-party callees), and the 12 findings carrying no
            METHOD_CALLED role are self-calls within net.cumba.corej.core.

            NullAway already enforces exactly this contract over exactly that surface, with a
            COMPLETE JSpecify implementation, and more strictly than SpotBugs is being asked
            to: the NullAway profile (active unless -Dnullaway.disabled) sets
            -Xep:NullAway:ERROR with JSpecifyMode=true, AcknowledgeRestrictiveAnnotations=true
            and AnnotatedPackages=net.cumba, and the build is green under it. So this pattern
            is duplicate coverage from an admittedly partial implementation, and the complete
            checker is the one to trust. Because the callee set is entirely net.cumba, the
            suppressed set is a strict SUBSET of NullAway's domain; nothing is hidden that
            NullAway does not already see.

            THE SUBSET PROPERTY IS A MEASUREMENT, NOT AN INVARIANT THIS FILTER ENFORCES.
            A SpotBugs Match cannot say "only when the callee is outside net.cumba", so this
            entry is unconditional and will also silence the one category SpotBugs would
            genuinely add over NullAway: a null-returning callee OUTSIDE net.cumba, which
            NullAway treats as non-null because the package is unannotated. Two things bound
            that risk today: the measured count of such findings is ZERO, and 4.9.8.3 (whose
            NP dataflow does not depend on JSpecify at all) found zero findings repo-wide in
            this codebase.

            SO: RE-RUN THE CALLEE CLASSIFICATION ON EVERY SpotBugs BUMP, not just this one.
            The check is: for each NP_NULL_ON_SOME_PATH_FROM_RETURN_VALUE in every
            target/spotbugsXml.xml, read the Method element whose role is METHOD_CALLED and
            confirm its classname starts with net.cumba. If any does not, that finding is NOT
            covered by NullAway and must be triaged on its own merits before this filter is
            kept.

            Revisit if SpotBugs completes its JSpecify implementation, or if NullAway is ever
            removed or demoted below ERROR.
        -->
        <Bug pattern="NP_NULL_ON_SOME_PATH_FROM_RETURN_VALUE" />
    </Match>

    <Match>
        <!--
            Also new in 4.10.x. Both occurrences in this repo are the same intentional shape:
            a nested factory widening a protected SPI accessor to public so the enclosing
            class can drive it - FunctionRegistry$ProviderFactory.getSuppliers and
            ServiceReportManager$SupplierFactory.getSuppliers, each a bare
            `return super.getSuppliers();`. Widening on override is the point; nothing that
            was meant to stay hidden is exposed. Both re-verified present in
            cumba-oss-corej-core on 2026-09-02.
        -->
        <Bug pattern="IAOM_DO_NOT_INCREASE_METHOD_ACCESSIBILITY" />
    </Match>

</FindBugsFilter>
